---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Personal notes, philosophy on slow creative work, and how I spend my days."
>
  <div class="max-w-[720px] mx-auto space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="space-y-3 pb-8 border-b border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800/80">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">
        About Me
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 font-serif leading-relaxed">
        I am a writer, programmer, and everyday tinkerer. I spend my days building small, gentle software and thinking about how to live well in an increasingly hurried world.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Bio Sections with Editorial Typography -->
    <section class="article-prose space-y-6">
      <h2>A Quiet Corner of the Web</h2>
      <p>
        I started this website because I missed the early, personal web—a time before feeds were dominated by engagement algorithms, outrage farming, and monetized influencer personas.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is my digital garden. It is a place where I can publish complete thoughts, post photographs taken on weekend walks, share notes on books that moved me, and document tools I built simply because I wanted them to exist.
      </p>

      <h2>A Few Things That Bring Me Joy</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Early morning silence:</strong> Making a hot cup of sencha or pour-over coffee before the world wakes up.</li>
        <li><strong>Second-hand bookstores:</strong> Spending an hour browsing dusty shelves for forgotten editions with hand-written inscriptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Unscheduled walks:</strong> Leaving my phone on the desk and wandering through residential neighborhoods and parks.</li>
        <li><strong>Small, local software:</strong> Writing little scripts and tools that run entirely on my machine without accounts or subscriptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Fountain pens & paper:</strong> The physical feedback of ink meeting cream-colored paper in a traveler's notebook.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Guiding Values</h2>
      <p>
        Whether I am writing an essay or writing software, I try to hold on to a few simple principles:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Slowness is a virtue:</strong> Good things take time to ferment. It is better to write one thoughtful essay a month than to publish thirty hasty reactions to the daily news cycle.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Own your tools:</strong> Choose plain text, local files, and durable formats that will still be accessible twenty years from now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resist the urge to scale:</strong> Not everything needs to be a business, a brand, or an audience of thousands. Doing something with deep care for a handful of people is deeply fulfilling.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Currently</h2>
      <p>
        Lately, I have been:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Reading essays by Wendell Berry and Ursula K. Le Guin.</li>
        <li>Experimenting with 35mm black-and-white film photography.</li>
        <li>Learning how to bake sourdough bread with an active starter.</li>
        <li>Writing reflections on slow technology and creative independence.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Say Hello</h2>
      <p>
        I love receiving thoughtful messages from other humans. If something I wrote resonated with you, or if you just want to say hi, send a note to <code class="font-mono text-xs">mando [at] mando.dev</code>. I read and reply to every message.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
